//
// TRAIN is the "known language" corpus: letter and bigram frequencies and the word list come from it.
// TEST is kept apart and only used to draw ciphertexts for the experiments, so the attack is never
// scored on text it has already seen. SAMPLES are short texts for the interactive demo.

export const TRAIN = [
  `The town wakes up slowly. Before six o'clock the only sound is the baker's van turning into the narrow street behind the church, and the soft rattle of metal shutters as the first shops open. By seven the cafe on the corner has put its chairs outside, even though the air is still cold and nobody wants to sit there yet. An old man walks his dog along the river, stopping every few steps so that the dog can read the news written in the grass. Children appear next, carrying bags that look too heavy for them, and their parents follow with keys in one hand and coffee in the other. The bus to the city leaves at half past seven, and everyone knows that it never waits. If you miss it, you wait forty minutes for the next one, and you arrive late to work with a story that your boss has already heard many times. On market days the square fills with stalls selling cheese, honey, vegetables and cheap shoes. The farmers who come down from the hills speak quickly and laugh loudly, and they always seem to know who is getting married and who has sold a house. By noon the town is busy and warm, and it is hard to believe that only a few hours earlier it was so quiet.`,

  `People have been making bread for thousands of years, and the basic recipe has hardly changed. You need flour, water, salt and something to make the dough rise. In the earliest times that something was simply time itself: wild yeasts floating in the air would settle on a wet mixture of ground grain, and after a day or two the dough would begin to bubble. Bakers learned to keep a piece of each batch to start the next one, and some families passed the same starter from parent to child for generations. In many villages there was a single large oven, and each household brought its loaves on a fixed day of the week. The baker marked every loaf with a small sign so that it could be returned to the right family. Bread was so important that its price was often controlled by law, and a sudden rise in the cost of grain could lead to anger in the streets. Today most bread is made in factories, where machines mix, shape and bake thousands of loaves every hour. Yet small bakeries have become popular again, because many people believe that slow fermentation gives better flavour and a crust that actually crackles when you press it.`,

  `A river is never in a hurry, but it is never still. Over thousands of years it cuts through rock, carries sand and stones down from the mountains, and builds wide flat plains near the sea. Near its source a river is usually narrow and fast. The water jumps over boulders, forms small waterfalls and digs a deep, steep valley shaped like the letter V. Further down, the slope becomes gentle, and the river starts to wander from side to side. Each bend grows a little every year, because the water flows faster on the outside of the curve and wears away the bank, while on the inside it slows down and drops the mud it was carrying. Sometimes a bend becomes so large that the river breaks through the narrow neck of land and takes a shorter path, leaving behind a quiet lake in the shape of a horseshoe. When heavy rain falls, a river can rise quickly and flood the fields around it. Farmers have always feared these floods, but they also know that the water leaves behind a layer of rich soil. That is why so many of the oldest cities in the world were built on the banks of large rivers.`,

  `Dear Martha, I am sorry it has taken me so long to write. The move was harder than I expected, and for the first few weeks I could not find anything, not even a clean cup. The new flat is small but bright, and from the kitchen window I can see a park with tall trees and a pond where children sail toy boats on Sunday mornings. The neighbours seem friendly. The woman who lives downstairs brought me a cake on the second day, and her son helped me carry the heaviest boxes up the stairs. Work is going well, although the office is much louder than the old one and I still get lost in the corridors. I have started taking the train instead of driving, and I read on the way, which is something I never had time for before. I miss our long walks and our evenings on your balcony, talking until it was too dark to see each other's faces. Please come and visit when the weather is warmer. There is a little room with a window that looks over the gardens, and it is yours whenever you want it. Give my love to your parents, and tell your brother that I still have his umbrella. With love, Anna.`,

  `The game is played with an ordinary deck of fifty two cards and is best with four players sitting in pairs, partners facing each other across the table. At the start of each round the dealer shuffles the cards and gives thirteen to every player, one at a time, moving to the left. Each player looks at their hand without showing it to anyone and decides how many tricks they think they can win. The player to the left of the dealer leads the first card, and the others must follow suit if they can. If a player has no card of the suit that was led, they may play any other card, including a trump. The highest card of the suit led wins the trick, unless a trump has been played, in which case the highest trump wins. The winner of each trick leads the next one. When all thirteen tricks have been played, the partners add up the tricks they have won and compare the total with their prediction. A pair that makes exactly what it promised earns ten points for every trick, while a pair that fails loses the same amount. The first pair to reach five hundred points wins the game, and the losers usually demand another one.`,

  `For twenty years Thomas lived alone on the rock, with only the light, the wind and the gulls for company. Every evening, as the sun went down behind the grey hills on the mainland, he climbed the one hundred and twelve steps of the tower and lit the great lamp. Every morning he climbed them again to put it out, clean the glass and write a few lines in the log book. Most entries were short. Wind from the west, calm sea, two ships passed in the night. But some were longer, and those were the nights he remembered. He remembered the winter storm that tore the roof off the storehouse and the fishing boat that came too close to the rocks, its crew shouting at him through the rain as if he could do something more than keep the light burning. He did keep it burning, and in the morning the boat was gone, safe in the harbour on the other side of the bay. A supply boat came once a fortnight with food, letters and newspapers that were already old. Thomas read every page, even the advertisements. When the light was finally made automatic, a man from the city came to tell him that he was no longer needed. Thomas packed one small bag and did not look back as the boat pulled away.`,

  `If you want to grow vegetables for the first time, start small. A single bed about the size of a door is enough to give you salad, herbs and a few tomatoes all through the summer, and it will not take over your weekends. Choose a place that gets at least six hours of sun a day, because almost every vegetable needs light more than anything else. Before you plant, dig the soil to the depth of a spade and mix in plenty of compost. Good soil should feel loose and dark and should smell like a forest after rain. Sow seeds in short rows and water them gently, so that they are not washed away. When the young plants appear, pull out the weakest ones and leave enough space for the others to grow. It feels cruel, but crowded plants will all stay small. Water in the morning rather than the evening, when the leaves can dry in the sun and are less likely to rot. Watch for slugs, which love tender leaves and can destroy a whole row in one night. Above all, be patient. Some things will fail, and that is normal. Every season you will learn something, and every year your garden will be a little better than the last.`,

  `A honey bee colony is a family of tens of thousands of insects that behaves almost like a single animal. At its heart is the queen, who does nothing but lay eggs, sometimes more than a thousand in a day. Around her are the workers, all female, who do every other job in the hive. Young workers clean the cells, feed the larvae and build new comb from wax that they produce in their own bodies. Older workers guard the entrance, and the oldest of all fly out to collect nectar, pollen and water. A forager may visit hundreds of flowers on a single trip, and when she finds a good source she returns to the hive and performs a dance on the comb. The angle of the dance shows the direction of the flowers compared with the sun, and its length tells the others how far they must fly. The males, called drones, have only one purpose, which is to mate with a queen from another colony. In autumn, when food becomes scarce, the workers push them out of the hive. Through the winter the bees cluster together in a tight ball, shivering their wing muscles to keep warm, and they live on the honey they stored during the summer.`,

  `The night train left the station a few minutes after ten. I had a narrow bed in a compartment with three strangers: a student going home for the holidays, a quiet man in a dark suit and an elderly woman who knitted without stopping until the lights went out. For a while nobody spoke. Then the student offered everyone some chocolate, and the woman told us that she had taken this same train every winter for almost forty years, to visit her sister who lived by the sea. She remembered when the carriages were made of wood and heated by a stove in the corridor. The man in the suit said that he sold farm machinery and travelled so much that he sometimes woke up without knowing which country he was in. I fell asleep listening to the steady rhythm of the wheels and woke only once, at a dark station where men with lanterns walked along the platform checking the brakes. When I opened the curtain in the morning, the landscape had completely changed. Instead of flat fields there were mountains covered in snow, and small wooden houses with smoke rising straight up from their chimneys into a perfectly clear sky.`,

  `A public library is one of the few places left where you can stay as long as you like without buying anything. Anyone may walk in, sit down at a table, read a newspaper, use a computer or simply escape the rain. In most towns the library also lends books, films and music for free, and many of them organise reading groups, language classes and story hours for small children. Behind the quiet rooms there is a great deal of work. Librarians choose which books to buy, repair damaged ones and remove those that nobody has borrowed for many years. Every item must be described and given a number, so that it can be placed on the right shelf and found again by the next reader. The most common system groups books by subject, giving each broad field a range of numbers and then dividing it into narrower topics. History, for example, sits far from cookery, while books about the same country stand side by side. In recent years libraries have had to change quickly. They now offer electronic books and online courses, and some even lend tools, seeds and musical instruments. Yet their main purpose has stayed the same: to make knowledge free and available to everyone.`,

  `My grandmother made the same soup every Friday, and the smell filled the whole building by midday. She started early in the morning by putting a large pot of water on the stove with an onion, two carrots, a stick of celery and a few bay leaves. While it heated, she chopped the rest of the vegetables on an old wooden board that had a deep groove worn into the middle. Potatoes went in first, because they took the longest to cook, then beans that had soaked in a bowl all night, then cabbage and a handful of green peas. She never measured anything. If you asked her how much salt to add, she would look at you as if the question made no sense and say that you simply add enough. When the vegetables were soft, she broke dry pasta into small pieces and dropped it into the pot, stirring slowly with a long spoon. At the very end she added a spoonful of olive oil and a little grated cheese. We ate the soup with thick slices of bread, and there was always enough for a second bowl. Years later I tried to make it myself, following my memory of her movements, and it was good, but it was never quite the same.`,

  `Clouds are made of tiny drops of water or crystals of ice, so small and light that they float in the air. They form when warm, moist air rises and cools, until the water vapour it carries can no longer stay invisible. The shape of a cloud tells you a lot about the weather that is coming. High, thin clouds that look like strands of white hair often appear a day before a change, as warm air slides over cold air far above the ground. Low grey sheets that cover the whole sky usually bring light rain or drizzle that can last for hours. The most dramatic clouds are the tall towers that build up on hot summer afternoons. Their bases are flat and dark, while their tops can climb so high that they spread out into a wide anvil. Inside them, strong winds carry drops up and down until they grow heavy enough to fall as heavy rain or even hail, and the movement of charges between the drops produces lightning. Sailors and farmers learned to read the sky long before there were weather forecasts on television, and many of their old sayings are still surprisingly accurate. A red sky in the evening, for instance, often means that dry air is moving in from the west.`,

  `We arrived at the museum just as the doors opened, hoping to see the most famous rooms before the crowds. The entrance hall was enormous, with a glass roof and a staircase that divided in two halfway up. A guide handed us a map, but it was so complicated that we soon folded it away and simply wandered. In the first gallery there were ancient pots and tools, each with a small label explaining where it had been found. Some of the pots still carried the fingerprints of the people who had made them, pressed into the clay thousands of years ago. Further on we found a room full of portraits. The faces stared down at us from heavy golden frames, serious men in black coats and women in silk dresses holding flowers or small dogs. My favourite painting was a small one in a corner, showing a girl reading a letter by a window. The light falling across her face looked so real that I wanted to step into the picture and read over her shoulder. By lunchtime our feet were tired and our heads were full. We sat in the courtyard eating sandwiches and agreed that we would need at least three more visits to see everything properly.`,

  `I learned to swim late, at the age of thirty, and I was more afraid than I had ever admitted. As a child I had always stayed at the edge of the water, pretending I preferred to read on the beach. The instructor at the local pool was a calm young woman who never raised her voice. In the first lesson she did not ask me to swim at all. She asked me to stand in the shallow end, put my face in the water and blow bubbles, again and again, until it felt boring rather than frightening. In the second lesson I learned to float on my back while she held one hand under my head. It took several weeks before I trusted the water enough to let go. Then, slowly, everything began to make sense. I learned to kick from the hips instead of the knees, to reach forward with long strokes and to turn my head to breathe without lifting it too high. The day I swam a full length without stopping, the instructor clapped and a few old men in the next lane cheered. Now I swim three mornings a week before work. It is the quietest part of my day, just the water, the black line on the bottom and the sound of my own breathing.`,

  `On Saturday mornings the market takes over the main street from one end to the other. The first stalls are set up before dawn by the light of lamps hung from their roofs. Fruit sellers build careful pyramids of oranges and apples, and the fishmonger lays his catch on beds of crushed ice, still shining from the sea. There is a man who sells only potatoes, eleven different kinds, and he will tell you exactly which one to use for frying, boiling or baking. Next to him a young couple sell goat cheese and yoghurt from their small farm, offering little pieces on toothpicks to anyone who slows down. Further along there are flowers, olives, spices in open sacks, second hand books and a woman who repairs watches while you wait. The noise is constant. Sellers call out their prices, customers argue politely, and a street musician plays the same three songs on an accordion every week. By eleven o'clock the best things are gone, and by one the stalls are packed up and the street is swept clean. Only a few cabbage leaves in the gutter show that, for a few hours, the town was full of colour, voices and the smell of fresh bread.`,

  `Building a small wooden boat by hand takes patience, a dry workshop and a long winter. The first step is to draw the shape of the hull at full size on large sheets of paper, a process that boat builders call lofting. From these drawings you cut a series of moulds, which look like the ribs of a skeleton, and set them up upside down on a strong frame. Next comes the keel, the long backbone that runs along the bottom of the boat. It is usually made of oak, because oak is hard, heavy and resists rot. Then the planks are bent around the moulds one by one, starting from the keel and working up towards the top edge. To make the wood flexible, builders often steam it in a long box until it can be bent without cracking. Each plank is fixed to the next with copper nails or glue, and every joint must be tight enough to keep the water out. When the hull is finished, it is turned over, the moulds are removed and the inside is fitted with seats and a deck. Finally the whole boat is sanded, painted and varnished. On the day it touches the water for the first time, the builder usually holds their breath, waiting to see if it floats level.`,

  `On a clear night far from the lights of any city, the sky is so full of stars that it is hard to find the familiar shapes among them. Across the middle runs a pale band of light, which is the edge of our own galaxy seen from the inside. Each star is a sun, many of them much larger and brighter than ours, but so far away that their light has travelled for years or even centuries before reaching our eyes. The planets look like stars too, but they do not twinkle as much, and if you watch them over several weeks you can see them move slowly against the background. People have always looked for patterns in the stars. Different cultures saw hunters, bears, ships and rivers, and told stories to explain how they got there. These patterns were also practical. They helped farmers decide when to plant, and they guided sailors across the open sea long before there were compasses or satellites. Today we know far more about what the stars are made of and how they are born and die, yet the feeling of standing alone under a dark sky has not changed. It still makes most of us feel very small, and strangely calm.`,

  `The home team started the match nervously, and for the first twenty minutes they hardly managed to keep the ball. The visitors pressed high, closed every space in midfield and almost scored when their captain hit the post with a powerful shot from outside the area. Slowly, however, the home side found their rhythm. Their young winger began to run at the defenders, and after half an hour he crossed from the left for the striker to head the ball into the corner of the net. The crowd, which had been quiet until then, suddenly came alive. Just before half time the visitors equalised from a corner, when nobody marked their tall central defender. The second half was open and exciting. Both goalkeepers made several fine saves, and the referee showed four yellow cards as the tackles became harder. With ten minutes left the home coach brought on two fresh players, and the decision paid off almost at once. One of them won a free kick near the edge of the box, and the other curled it over the wall and past the diving keeper. The visitors attacked desperately in the final minutes, but the home team held on to win by two goals to one.`,

  `We left the village at dawn, when the grass was still wet and the mountains were only dark shapes against a pale sky. The path climbed steeply through a forest of pine trees, and for the first hour nobody talked much. We could hear only our own breathing, the crunch of our boots and, somewhere below, the sound of a stream. Above the trees the landscape opened up. Wide meadows full of yellow and purple flowers stretched towards the rocky peaks, and here and there cows with bells around their necks lifted their heads to watch us pass. We stopped by a small lake for lunch. The water was so clear that we could count the stones on the bottom, and so cold that none of us dared to swim. In the afternoon the weather began to change. Clouds gathered around the summit, the wind grew stronger and the temperature dropped quickly. We decided to turn back instead of trying to reach the top, which was disappointing but wise. By the time we reached the village again, it was raining hard, and we sat in the warm kitchen of the guest house drinking tea and planning to try again the next summer.`,

  `Before the printing press, every book had to be copied by hand. A single copy of a long text could take a scribe many months, and books were therefore rare, expensive and owned mostly by churches, universities and rich families. In the middle of the fifteenth century, a goldsmith in Germany combined several existing ideas into a new machine. He made small metal letters that could be arranged into lines and pages, inked, pressed onto paper and then taken apart and used again. He adapted a press that had been used to make wine and olive oil, and he developed an oily ink that stuck to metal better than the inks used for writing. The results spread across Europe with remarkable speed. Within fifty years there were printing shops in hundreds of towns, and millions of books had been produced. Prices fell, more people learned to read, and ideas could travel far beyond the place where they first appeared. Scientists could compare their observations, and arguments about religion and politics could reach thousands of readers in a few weeks. Many historians consider the printing press one of the most important inventions of all time, because it changed not only how books were made but how people thought.`,

  `Cats have lived alongside humans for thousands of years, yet they have never quite agreed to become fully tame. They probably first moved into farming villages to hunt the mice that gathered around stores of grain, and people were happy to let them stay. Even today a house cat keeps many of the habits of its wild ancestors. It sleeps for most of the day and becomes active at dusk and dawn, which are the best times for hunting. It marks its territory by rubbing its face against furniture and the legs of its owners, leaving a scent that we cannot smell. It sharpens its claws on whatever surface it can find, often the most expensive chair in the room. Cats communicate in many subtle ways. A slow blink is a sign of trust, a tail held straight up means a friendly greeting, and a tail that swings quickly from side to side is a clear warning. Interestingly, adult cats rarely meow at each other. They seem to use that sound mainly to talk to people, perhaps because they have learned that it works. Anyone who has been woken at five in the morning by a hungry cat knows exactly how well it works.`,

  `After midnight the city becomes a different place. The offices are dark, the traffic thins out and the noise of the day fades into a low hum. Yet many people are still awake and working. Bakers are already mixing dough for the morning, nurses walk the quiet corridors of the hospital, and drivers steer empty buses along routes that seem much longer without passengers. In the train station a cleaner pushes a machine slowly across the shining floor, while a few travellers sleep on benches with their bags tied to their wrists. Near the river, a small restaurant stays open for taxi drivers and night workers, serving eggs, sausages and strong coffee until the sun comes up. Street lamps throw circles of yellow light on the pavement, and between them the shadows seem deeper than they really are. Foxes come out from the parks to search the bins, moving with surprising confidence as if the streets belonged to them, and at this hour perhaps they do. Then, around five o'clock, the first delivery vans appear, the windows of the flats begin to light up one by one, and the city slowly turns back into the busy, impatient place that everyone knows.`,

  `On her first day of school, Lucy wore a new blue coat and carried a bag with a picture of a horse on it. She had been excited for weeks, but as soon as she saw the large grey building and heard the noise of hundreds of children in the yard, she held her father's hand very tightly and refused to let go. A tall teacher with a kind face knelt down to speak to her. She asked Lucy her name and told her that there was a special peg in the classroom with her name written above it, waiting for her coat. That was enough. Lucy let go of her father's hand and followed the teacher inside without looking back, and her father stood by the gate for a long time, feeling strangely abandoned. The classroom was bright and full of colour. There were drawings on the walls, a corner with cushions and books, and a big window where a hamster lived in a cage. The children sat on a carpet and each said their name and their favourite food. At the end of the day Lucy ran out with paint on her sleeve and a paper crown on her head, talking so fast that nobody could understand a single word.`,

  `Most adults need between seven and nine hours of sleep a night, yet many of us regularly get much less. We stay up late to finish work, watch one more episode of a series or scroll through our phones, and then we wake up tired and reach for coffee. Scientists have shown that the cost of this habit is higher than it seems. During sleep the brain sorts and stores the memories of the day, which is why students who sleep after studying remember more than those who stay awake all night. Sleep also helps the body repair muscles, control appetite and fight infections. People who sleep badly for long periods have a greater risk of many illnesses, and they are more likely to have accidents at work or on the road. Fortunately, a few simple changes can help. Going to bed and getting up at the same time every day, even at weekends, trains the body clock. A dark, cool and quiet bedroom makes it easier to fall asleep, and so does avoiding bright screens in the last hour before bed. Exercise during the day is good, but heavy meals and strong drinks late in the evening are not. Above all, sleep should be treated as a need, not as a luxury.`,

  `In winter the village is cut off for days at a time. The road that climbs from the valley twists through dozens of tight bends, and after a heavy snowfall it can take the plough a whole morning to clear it. The people who live here are used to it. Every autumn they fill their cellars with potatoes, apples, cheese and wood for the stove, and they keep candles in every drawer in case the electricity fails. The small school has only nine pupils, of all ages, who learn together in a single room heated by an old iron stove. When the snow is deep the older children ski to school, and the teacher lets them leave early if another storm is coming. In the evenings families visit each other, play cards and tell the same stories they have told for years, laughing at the same moments. On Sundays almost everyone goes to the little church, not always to pray but to see who is there and to hear the news. Tourists come for a few weeks in February to ski on the slopes above the village, filling the two small hotels. Then they leave again, and the village returns to its own quiet rhythm until spring.`,

  `Paolo has been a fisherman for more than forty years, like his father and his grandfather before him. He gets up at three in the morning, drinks a small cup of black coffee standing in his kitchen, and walks down to the harbour while the town is still asleep. His boat is old and painted bright blue, with the name of his wife written on the side in white letters. He starts the engine, checks the nets and heads out towards the open sea, following the lights of the other boats. Fishing is not what it used to be. There are fewer fish every year, the rules are stricter, and the price of fuel keeps rising. Some days he comes back with a good catch of sardines, squid and the occasional large tuna, and other days he hardly covers his costs. Still, he cannot imagine doing anything else. He loves the silence of the sea before dawn, the moment when the sun appears over the water and the gulls start following the boat. When he returns, his wife is waiting at the market with crates and ice, and together they sell the catch before ten. In the afternoon he repairs the nets, sitting on the quay and talking with the other old fishermen about the weather.`,

  `The bicycle repair shop on Mill Street is so small that only two customers can stand inside at the same time. The walls are covered with tools hanging from nails, tyres of every size and old photographs of races from long ago. The owner, a thin man with grey hair and grease on his fingers, has been fixing bicycles here since he was a teenager. He can tell what is wrong with a bike simply by listening to it as you push it through the door. A clicking sound means a loose pedal, a squeak means the chain needs oil, and a grinding noise usually means that the brakes are worn out. He works quickly but never rushes, and he always explains what he is doing, as if every customer were an apprentice. Most repairs cost very little. He replaces a broken spoke, straightens a bent wheel or adjusts the gears in a few minutes, and he often refuses to charge children at all. In the window there are usually two or three second hand bicycles that he has rebuilt from old parts, each one cleaned and polished until it shines. They sell within days, mostly to students who cannot afford a new bike and who come back every year for a check up.`,

  `The afternoon had been hot and heavy, with no wind at all, and even the birds seemed too tired to sing. Around four o'clock the light changed. The sky to the west turned a strange yellow colour, then dark purple, and a cool breeze suddenly lifted the curtains in the open window. We heard the first thunder far away, a long low roll like furniture being moved in an upstairs room. Within minutes the storm was above us. Lightning lit up the garden in brilliant white flashes, and the thunder that followed was so loud and so close that the glasses on the shelf trembled. Then the rain came, not in drops but in grey sheets that hammered on the roof and turned the path into a small river. The dog hid under the bed, and the children pressed their faces against the window, counting the seconds between each flash and each crash to see how far away the lightning was. Half an hour later it was all over. The clouds moved east, the sun came out again, and the whole garden was shining and dripping. The air smelled of wet earth and cut grass, and it felt cool and fresh for the first time in days.`,

  `Every year on the last Sunday of the summer, the whole family meets at my aunt's house in the country for a long dinner in the garden. Nobody remembers exactly when the tradition started, but nobody would dream of missing it. The preparations begin days in advance. My aunt plans the menu, my uncle cleans the old wooden table that lives in the barn for the rest of the year, and the cousins argue about who will sit next to whom. On the day itself the guests arrive throughout the afternoon, each bringing a dish, a bottle or a new baby to show off. There are always more people than chairs, so the children sit on the grass with their plates on their knees. The meal goes on for hours. There are salads, roasted vegetables, a huge dish of pasta, grilled meat and fish, and at least four different cakes. Conversations move from politics to football to old family stories that everyone has heard before but still enjoys. As the sun goes down, someone lights the lanterns hanging in the trees, and my grandfather, who is now ninety three, stands up to make the same short speech he makes every year, thanking everyone for coming.`,

  `Every city has a place where lost things end up, and in ours it is a long room under the central station. Shelves run from the floor to the ceiling, and on them lie thousands of objects that people have left behind on trains, buses and platforms. There are hundreds of umbrellas, of course, in every colour, along with gloves, scarves, hats, books, phones and keys. But there are stranger things too. Over the years the staff have received a wedding dress, a set of false teeth, a stuffed fox, a violin and, once, a small boat. Each item is given a number and a label with the date and place it was found. If nobody claims it within three months, it is sold at an auction or given to a charity. The people who work there say that the best part of the job is the moment someone recovers something they thought was gone forever. An old man once came in looking for a photograph of his late wife that he had left on a bench. It took two hours to find it, in a box of papers, and when the clerk handed it to him he cried and shook her hand for a full minute before he could say thank you.`,

  `Airports are strange places where time seems to follow its own rules. People eat breakfast at midnight, sleep on rows of plastic chairs and buy perfume they do not need simply because they have an hour to spare. The journey through an airport follows the same steps everywhere in the world. First you check in and hand over your suitcase, watching it disappear on a moving belt and hoping you will see it again. Then you queue for the security check, where you remove your belt, your shoes and every liquid you are carrying, and place them in grey trays. After that comes the long walk past shops and restaurants to the gate, which is always the furthest one from where you started. Finally you wait, staring at the screen until the word boarding appears next to your flight. On the plane, passengers squeeze into narrow seats, fasten their belts and pretend to listen to the safety instructions. For a few hours they are suspended between two places, belonging to neither, and when the wheels finally touch the ground, many of them feel a small, private relief.`,

  `Chess is played on a square board of sixty four squares, coloured light and dark in turn. Each player begins with sixteen pieces: one king, one queen, two rooks, two bishops, two knights and eight pawns. Every piece moves in its own way. The rook travels in straight lines along rows and columns, the bishop moves diagonally, and the queen combines the powers of both, which makes her the strongest piece on the board. The knight jumps in the shape of the letter L and is the only piece that can leap over others. Pawns move forward one square at a time, but they capture diagonally, and a pawn that reaches the far side of the board can be promoted to any other piece, usually a queen. The aim of the game is not to capture the king but to trap it, so that it is attacked and cannot escape. This is called checkmate, and it ends the game at once. Beginners often think that chess is about calculating many moves ahead, but strong players rely just as much on patterns they have learned from thousands of earlier games, recognising good positions almost at a glance.`,

  `Every Saturday morning I volunteer at an animal shelter on the edge of town. The building is old and the heating rarely works, but the staff are cheerful and the animals are always pleased to see us. My first job is to clean the kennels, which is cold, wet and not very pleasant, but it has to be done before anything else. Then I fill the water bowls, prepare the food and take the dogs out for a walk in the field behind the shelter, two or three at a time. Some of them pull so hard on the lead that I can barely keep up, while others are so nervous that they hide behind my legs whenever a car passes. There is one old grey dog called Max who has been at the shelter for almost two years. Nobody seems to want him because he is slow and has a bad leg, but he is the gentlest animal I have ever met. Every week I hope that someone will come and give him a home. When a dog is finally adopted, the whole staff gathers at the door to say goodbye, and more than one of us usually goes back inside with wet eyes.`,

  `Tea is the most popular drink in the world after water, and every culture that drinks it seems to have its own ritual. All true tea comes from the leaves of a single plant, a kind of evergreen bush that grows best on misty hillsides in warm climates. The differences between green, black and white tea come from what happens after the leaves are picked. For green tea the leaves are heated soon after picking, which stops them from changing colour and keeps a fresh, grassy taste. For black tea they are rolled and left in the air until they turn dark, which gives a stronger flavour. In some countries tea is drunk plain from small cups, in others with milk and sugar, and in others again with mint, lemon, spices or even butter and salt. Making a good cup is simple but not careless. Use fresh water, do not let delicate teas sit in boiling water for too long, and give the leaves enough room to open. Many people say that the best tea is not the most expensive one, but the one shared with good company on a cold afternoon.`,

  `When I was a boy, my grandfather kept an old radio on a shelf in his workshop. It was a large wooden box with a round dial and two brown knobs, and it took almost a minute to warm up after he switched it on. While we waited, the speaker hissed and crackled, and then, slowly, voices and music would rise out of the noise as if from very far away. My grandfather liked to turn the dial very carefully, searching for distant stations. Sometimes we heard men speaking languages we could not understand, sometimes an orchestra playing, sometimes only the sound of the wind between the stations. He told me that at night the signals could travel much further, bouncing off a layer high in the sky, and that once he had heard a ship calling for help in the middle of the ocean. I never knew whether that story was true. When he died, the radio came to me. It no longer works, but I keep it on a shelf in my own house, and sometimes I still turn the dial, just to hear the soft click of the old knob.`,

  `A glacier is a huge mass of ice that moves slowly over the land, pulled downhill by its own enormous weight. It forms in places where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer, so that year after year the snow piles up, presses down on the layers below and gradually turns into solid ice. Although ice looks hard and still, a glacier flows like an extremely thick liquid, sometimes only a few centimetres a day and sometimes several metres. As it moves, it scrapes the ground beneath it, carrying away rocks of every size and grinding them into fine powder. When the ice finally melts, it leaves behind wide valleys with steep sides, long ridges of stones and lakes of a beautiful milky blue colour. Many of the most famous landscapes in the world were shaped in this way during the last ice age. Today most glaciers are shrinking quickly as the climate becomes warmer. In some mountain regions, people depend on the water that flows from melting ice during the dry summer months, and scientists are worried about what will happen when the glaciers are gone.`,

  `I arrived at the interview twenty minutes early and spent most of that time in the bathroom, checking my hair and practising my answers in a whisper. The office was on the ninth floor of a glass building, and the receptionist asked me to wait on a white sofa that was so low my knees were almost level with my chin. When my name was called, I followed a young man along a corridor into a small room where three people sat behind a long table. They were polite but serious, and for the first few minutes my voice sounded strange and thin, as if it belonged to someone else. They asked why I wanted the job, what I knew about the company and how I had dealt with a difficult problem in the past. Slowly I began to relax. One of them smiled at a joke I made about my first job in a pizza restaurant, and after that the conversation flowed more easily. At the end they asked if I had any questions, and I asked about the team I would be working with. Two days later they called to offer me the position. I said yes before they had even finished the sentence.`,

  `In late summer the fields around the village turn yellow with sunflowers, millions of them standing in long straight rows as far as the eye can see. When the plants are young, their heads follow the sun across the sky during the day, facing east in the morning and west in the evening, and then turning back again during the night. Once the flowers are fully open, they stop moving and most of them simply face east, towards the morning light. Farmers grow them mainly for their seeds, which are pressed to make oil for cooking or roasted and sold as snacks. The work begins in spring, when the soil is prepared and the seeds are planted in warm ground. During the summer the farmers watch the sky anxiously, because a storm with hail can destroy a whole field in a few minutes. In autumn, when the heads have dried and turned brown, large machines move slowly across the fields, cutting the plants and separating the seeds. Tourists often stop their cars by the side of the road to take photographs of the flowers, but few of them come back to see the bare, brown fields after the harvest.`,

  `Most of us check our phones dozens of times a day, often without even noticing. We pick them up when we wake, while we wait for the bus, during meals and even in the middle of conversations with friends. Each small message or notification gives the brain a tiny reward, and over time the habit becomes almost automatic. There is nothing wrong with technology itself. Phones help us stay in touch with family far away, find our way in strange cities and look up almost anything in seconds. The problem is that constant interruptions make it harder to concentrate on a single task for a long time, and many people say that they feel more anxious and tired than before. Some have started to fight back with simple rules. They leave the phone in another room at night, turn off most notifications, or keep one day a week completely free of screens. Others replace the habit with something else, such as carrying a book or a notebook in their pocket. Those who try these changes often report the same surprise: once the first restless days have passed, they do not miss their phones nearly as much as they expected.`,

  `Every August, when I was a child, we spent two weeks in a small wooden house by a lake in the hills. There was no television and the nearest shop was an hour's walk away, so we spent our days outside from morning until dark. My sister and I swam in the lake before breakfast, when the water was smooth as glass and a thin mist floated above its surface. Later we built dams in the stream, caught small fish with a net and climbed the rocks above the shore to jump into the deep water below. In the afternoons, when it was too hot to do anything else, we lay in the shade reading comics that we had already read a hundred times. My father fished from an old rowing boat and almost never caught anything, but he did not seem to mind. In the evenings we cooked on a fire outside, and we stayed up late watching the bats fly over the water and listening to the frogs. When it was time to leave, we always cried in the back of the car, and we started counting the days until the next summer before we had even reached the main road.`,
];

